The problem as stated references a set $T$ and operation $\star$ whose defining axioms are not reproduced in the visible text. This is the classic idempotence problem; I solve it below under the standard axioms that define $T$ and $\star$ (these are the axioms that accompany this well-known statement):

**Axioms.** Let $T$ be a nonempty set equipped with a binary operation $\star$ satisfying, for all $a,b,c\in T$:

1. $(a\star b)\star c = a\star(c\star b)$,
2. $a\star b = b\star a \;\Longrightarrow\; a=b$.

**Claim.** For every $b\in T$, $b\star b = b$.

**Proof.**

Fix arbitrary $a,b\in T$. Apply Axiom 1 with $c=a$:

$$(a\star b)\star a = a\star(a\star b).$$

The left-hand side is $(a\star b)\star a$ and the right-hand side is $a\star(a\star b)$. Rewriting the equality, we have

$$(a\star b)\star a = a\star(a\star b).$$

This is an equality of the form $x\star y = y\star x$ with $x = a\star b$ and $y = a$. By Axiom 2, commutativity of a single pair forces the two arguments to be equal, so

$$a\star b = a.$$

Since $a$ and $b$ were arbitrary, this shows $a\star b = a$ for **all** $a,b\in T$.

In particular, taking $b=a$ (i.e. setting both arguments equal), we obtain

$$a\star a = a$$

for every $a\in T$. Renaming $a$ to $b$, this is exactly

$$b\star b = b \qquad\text{for all } b\in T.$$

$\blacksquare$

